                 VERSION 1 REVISION 91 -- BUILT ON 23 Nov 94
              *************************************************

Added a loging feature to the device.  There is a new keyword that can be
specified for each configuration entry:  LOGFILE.  This names a file to be
written to each time a connection is dropped.  The information written to
the logfile is:

(Date Time) <port number> <unit number on port> <user name>@<host> (<host ip>)
	<connect time> <connect date> <bytes received> <bytes transmitted>

(All on one line, of course.)

<user name> is only available for rlogin connections, and will be blank for
telnet connections.

A sample configuration line that uses the LOGFILE keyword might be:

23 5 255.255.255.255 logfile inet:telnetd.log

More than one configuration entry can point at the same logfile.  The
device will internally lock around writes to the log to prevent the logfile
from being clobbered.  If you have OwnDevUnit.library installed, it will
also lock the logfile using that so that utilities such as the AmigaUUCP
"trimfile" command will not be able to interfere with log updates, either.
For this to work properly, however, you need at least version 2.1 of
OwnDevUnit.library.

-----

Added new keyword to the configuration file:  RLOGIND-8.  If specified,
units serviced by this port will run with a bastardized version of the
rlogin protocol were all options negotations are ignored and treated as
data.  This was done as an attempt to allow people running BBS' using a
NetBlazer as their modem rack to accept uploads without problems (the
NetBlazer's telnet is whacked when it comes to downloads, and there's
nothing I've been able to find that will get around this).

-----

Added new keyword to the configuration file:  ANSWERTIMEOUT.  This keyword
can be used to specify a maximum time (in seconds) a unit can "ring" for
before telnetd.device tries moving on to the next unit managed by the
supervisor. Consider what happens when the program monitoring a given unit
hangs and the person that was connected to it disconnects.  New connection
attempts will find that the unit is available, and start ringing it.
However, since the program has crashed, it will never answer the RING
messages.  Thus, the connect will appear to hang.  This keyword allows one to
specify a number of seconds that a unit must "answer" within.  If it does not
answer, the supervisor will try finding another unit to service the
connection and start ringing it.  Note: this keyword is useless for units
which are set to auto-answer.

-----

Fixed a bug in the receive routine that could crash telnetd.device,
particularily on heavilly loaded systems.  Thanks to Geoff and Mark at
Equinox Networks for providing the Enforcer hit information that helped
pinpoint the problem.
